The  driver  allows  a  PC  parallel printer port to be used as a
point‐to‐point network interface between two similarly configured
systems.  Data is transferred 4 bits at a time, using the printer
status lines for input: hence there is no requirement for special
bidirectional hardware and  any  standard  AT‐compatible  printer
port  with  working  interrupts  may  be  used.   During the boot
process, for each device which is attached and has  an  interrupt
capability,  a  corresponding  device is attached.  The device is
configured using using the options for a  point‐to‐point  network
interface:  Configuring a device with causes the corresponding to
be reserved for PLIP until the network  interface  is  configured
The communication protocol is selected by the flag: (default) Use
mode  (LPIP).  This is the simpler of the two modes and therefore
slightly  more  efficient.   Use  Crynwr/Linux  compatible   mode
(CLPIP).   This  mode has a simulated Ethernet packet header, and
is easier to interface to other types of equipment.   The  inter‐
face  MTU  defaults  to  1500, but may be set to any value.  Both
ends of the link must be configured with the same MTU.   See  for
details  on configuring network interfaces.  The cable connecting
the  two   parallel   ports   should   be   wired   as   follows:
        Pin     Pin     Description         2       15      Data0
‐>      ERROR*              3       13      Data1     ‐>     SLCT
        4       12      Data2 ‐> PE         5       10      Data3
‐>      ACK*              6       11      Data4      ‐>      BUSY
        15      2       ERROR*              ‐>              Data0
        13      3       SLCT                ‐>              Data1
        12      4       PE                  ‐>              Data2
